 ## Background
-Standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [Parent] uses isotropic or anisotropic Gaussians for view synthesis but lacks explicit geometric primitives for surface reconstruction. Recent extensions have addressed limitations: 4D Gaussian Splatting incorporates temporal dynamics, while 6D Gaussian Splatting models view-dependent effects by adding directional dimensions [S1]. Oriented Gaussian Splatting introduces normals for surface wrapping [9621-oriented-gaussian-splatting.md]. 3D Skew-Normal Splatting uses skew-normal distributions for asymmetry [10045-3d-skew-normal-splatting.md]. Barycentric Splat Parameterization binds Gaussians to meshes using barycentric coordinates [15283-barycentric-splat-parameterization.md]. Anisotropic covariance allows 3D Gaussians to scale along x, y, and z axes and rotate in 3D space to better fit scene geometry [11879-anisotropic-covariance.md]. In-plane rotation is used in mesh-constrained frameworks like OMeGa [S2].
+Standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [Parent] uses isotropic or anisotropic Gaussians for view synthesis but lacks explicit geometric primitives for surface reconstruction [G]. Recent extensions have addressed limitations: 4D Gaussian Splatting incorporates temporal dynamics, while 6D Gaussian Splatting models view-dependent effects by adding directional dimensions [S1]. Oriented Gaussian Splatting introduces normals for surface wrapping [9621-oriented-gaussian-splatting.md]. 3D Skew-Normal Splatting uses skew-normal distributions for asymmetry [10045-3d-skew-normal-splatting.md]. Barycentric Splat Parameterization binds Gaussians to meshes using barycentric coordinates [15283-barycentric-splat-parameterization.md]. Anisotropic covariance allows 3D Gaussians to scale along x, y, and z axes and rotate in 3D space to better fit scene geometry [11879-anisotropic-covariance.md]. In-plane rotation is used in mesh-constrained frameworks like OMeGa [S2].
 
